00:03:29.787 --> 00:03:31.620
PASCAL DALLA TORRE: So
basically, at Veolia,

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.100
the G Suite story
started in 2012.

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.100
Basically, we did this
study at that time

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:41.200
because we wanted to
challenge the solution we had,

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.170
which was Microsoft Office.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.160
So we did that study.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.210
It didn't take so much
time to realize that we

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:49.620
have to move to G Suite.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:55.330
So it started in 2013, with
whole core services, basically.

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:59.730
So we did a massive rollout
in all our business units.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:04.530
Just for information, we
have like 60 business units

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:06.040
all over the world.

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.830
So as I said before,
it's 160,000 people.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.380
So all are concerned
about that, even

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:16.470
though we don't have so many
user accounts, around 100,000.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.950
So we had three major
concerns at that time,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.980
which were 2-step verification.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:27.330
We wanted to roll out accounts
with a 2-step verification

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.780
because we are massively
using single sign-in,

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.950
and all our solutions
basically rely on the strength

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.130
of Google identity.

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:41.970
So it's mandatory for us to
have a single sign-in in place

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:43.410
and 2-step verification.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.560
And the third point is also a
tool that we have developed,

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.020
which is called a DIMA, for
Digital Identity Management.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.270
It's basically related
to all our accounts

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.700
and the lifecycle
of our employees.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.270
When they get into the
company, when they move.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:04.270
So it's the whole challenge that
we have to face at that time.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.690
And as for the
administration, we

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:11.350
are currently doing something
we call our technical debt.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:14.310
But we will talk about that
maybe a little bit later.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.100
And the idea behind it is that
since we massively rolled out

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:21.210
all those services, we didn't
pay so much attention just

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:22.600
with security at the beginning.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.630
So now we have to get rid
of these technical depth,

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.670
and we can detail
this in a few minutes.

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:35.140
RYAN SEIT: So at
Casper, we've got

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:39.790
approximately 600 to
700 people, far less

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.980
than both Red and Pascal here.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:45.500
But that said, they're
dispersed throughout the world.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:48.970
We have offices in
Berlin, Toronto, New York,

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:53.050
and San Francisco,
as well as stores all

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:58.000
throughout domestic US,
some in Canada as well.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:01.000
So supporting all those users
gets a little difficult.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.790
I would say the biggest thing
or the difficulty that we've

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:11.420
run into is, as a retail
company, with holidays,

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:13.040
you have to hire.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.380
And then there's a lot
of attrition in retail,

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:17.450
so a lot of user
accounts get provisioned

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:18.742
and then quickly deprovisioned.

00:06:18.742 --> 00:06:21.470
And so being able to turn
those accounts over quickly

00:06:21.470 --> 00:06:22.970
is very critical
for us, which we've

00:06:22.970 --> 00:06:25.610
been able to use G Suite for.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:28.400
Aside from that, we use all
the core G Suite functionality,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.410
such as drive, docs,
things like that,

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:32.870
to collaborate with all
of our users in real time.

00:06:32.870 --> 00:06:36.650
Previous to that, we were
using Office 365, which is OK.

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:38.900
But when you're trying to
collaborate on a last minute

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:40.820
presentation for
a board meeting,

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:43.160
it gets very difficult
in PowerPoint.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.730
So Slides makes
that a bit easier.

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:48.080
Past that, a day in
the life of myself--

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:51.770
I think we're covering
that at this point--

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:53.680
a day in the life
for us is we have

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:57.350
IT stand up in the morning,
starting with meditation.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:58.598
It's five minutes.

00:06:58.598 --> 00:07:00.890
The team looked at me like
I was crazy when we started,

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:02.510
but honestly, it's
super helpful just

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:05.660
to get in a good mindset for
the madness that will ensue

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:07.860
throughout the rest of the day.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.240
So we start off with our SLAs.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:14.270
We go through our roadmap for
the quarter, for the year,

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.340
to make sure we're all
adhering to the due dates

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.800
that we've agreed to.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:21.410
Meetings galore, as
I'm sure everybody

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:24.200
can attest to with all the
different cross-functional

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.720
partners that we have
to ensure that we're all

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.830
aligned on the rapidly moving
targets of the business.

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:31.430
And let's see.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.620
Unique security, I think
I already mentioned that

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:35.750
with the attrition in retail.

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:38.680
So that's about it.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:40.680
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ:
So with Globe,

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.423
we're actually about
almost 18,000 employees.

00:07:44.423 --> 00:07:46.340
We're scattered across
the Philippine islands.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:51.830
That's about 7,107, depending
on the tide, whether it's

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:53.780
rising or low tide.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:57.590
But we've been a
G Suite customer

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:59.760
for about six years now.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:01.710
And as I mentioned
earlier, we're

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:06.350
using quite heavily on G Suite,
specifically on productivity,

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:07.155
Drive.

00:08:07.155 --> 00:08:11.420
We're subscribed to
unlimited storage and all.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:18.270
Within those 17,000
workforce, majority of them--

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.910
it's probably around
9,000 regular employees.

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:23.430
The other are
temporary employees.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:27.050
So with that in mind, that would
be our security concerns as

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:30.810
well because we have an
unlimited storage of data,

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:32.960
and at the same time,
we're scattered everywhere.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.000
Since we're scattered,
a lot of our concerns

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:41.240
mostly in our devices and
our identity and making sure

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:45.117
it's self-service in
terms of troubleshooting

00:08:45.117 --> 00:08:45.950
all of these things.

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:52.080
And we're able to fix that
through MDM, G Suite MDM.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.560
And it's quite
weird because those

00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:00.380
that are not within
our head office

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:04.250
are actually the most to
adapt quickly, rather than

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:05.960
the ones in the head office.

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:10.220
Probably because they have
become self-reliant and making

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:11.910
sure that they're
following instructions

00:09:11.910 --> 00:09:12.910
and all of these things.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.160
Day in the life--

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:19.910
I've been working for
Globe for six years,

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:21.950
and I'm handling
apps and devices.

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:27.068
So every employee apps
and employee devices

00:09:27.068 --> 00:09:28.610
goes through me in
terms of approval,

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:32.820
in terms of the roadmaps and
all of those requirements.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.810
So we've been trying to
move everything to the cloud

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:37.320
and also to mobile-first.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:40.460
So that's what I do every
day-- and also coordinate

00:09:40.460 --> 00:09:42.090
with our security team.

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:45.290
We have our command center,
which basically monitors

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:48.470
all of our network traffic,
specifically handing

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:51.530
one of the largest
network infrastructure

00:09:51.530 --> 00:09:53.722
in the Philippines.

00:09:53.722 --> 00:09:54.680
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Great.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:56.975
So thinking about
this environment based

00:09:56.975 --> 00:09:59.600
on your own experience, there's
a few things I'll follow up on.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.530
But I'll ask the
big question first,

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:03.368
which is, if you're
a G Suite admin,

00:10:03.368 --> 00:10:04.910
what is one thing
you should do right

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:06.590
now to improve your security?

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:08.950
And we'll start
with you, Pascal.

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.620
PASCAL DALLA TORRE: Well,
as a cyber security officer,

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:16.340
I would say when you
roll out Google service,

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:18.310
whatever G Suite
service, you have

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:22.480
to think about the best
compromise between the best

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:27.410
experience, user experience,
and security risks.

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:28.810
So what does it mean?

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:31.210
If you don't do that,
what is going to happen

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:35.997
is you will generate what we
call a big technical debt.

00:10:35.997 --> 00:10:38.080
I'll give you some figures,
because as I mentioned

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.390
before, when we started
to roll out, massively,

00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:44.200
our G Suite services, we
pay attention to user-first.

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.780
And we said, OK, we want the
better experience for them

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:48.970
because we were also
convinced that it was the best

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:50.940
choice that we did.

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:54.310
But we didn't take care so much
about security at that time.

00:10:54.310 --> 00:10:56.580
OK, so now what we
realized is that, I'll

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:57.750
give you some figures.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:11:01.140
We have, for instance,
1,400 Google Groups

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.320
without any manager or owners.

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:05.400
So it means we have no
idea about the spams

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:07.110
for those groups, for instance.

00:11:07.110 --> 00:11:11.160
We have over 100 internal
domains which are not compliant

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:13.060
related to antispoofing.

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:15.030
So we have to work about that.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:19.530
We have also some figures
really tied to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:23.890
We have 1,000 users who still
benefit from this service.

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:26.490
So the idea behind it, if you
give you those figures, that

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:30.010
this huge technical debt,
we have to solve that.

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:33.300
So the best way to do it is,
of course, when you start,

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:37.260
is to put in place some
KPIs, automatic ones.

00:11:37.260 --> 00:11:39.150
Because then you put
a process and you're

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:41.040
able to trust what's
going to happen

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.320
and the evolution
about those services.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:47.430
Again, with a company like
ours, large-scale company,

00:11:47.430 --> 00:11:48.360
you've got to do that.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:52.680
Otherwise everything is
going to be out of control.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:56.220
And one other point is also
to take the do good measure

00:11:56.220 --> 00:11:58.278
to put the risk under control.

00:11:58.278 --> 00:11:59.820
And this is what
we've been doing now

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:01.590
for quite some months.

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:05.460
And it's hard and very
painful to go to business unit

00:12:05.460 --> 00:12:07.860
and to say, could you please
justify why you're still

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:09.720
using this [INAUDIBLE]?

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:11.790
Which, if you don't have
any business reasons,

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.550
you're not supposed to use that
anymore, at least from a user

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:15.820
perspective.

00:12:15.820 --> 00:12:18.650
So it's a long, long journey.

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:21.180
But one also point
I wouldn't mention

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:25.410
here, which is quite essential,
is to do it little by little.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:27.790
I know it takes quite a while.

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:29.640
But if you think
that you are going

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:33.310
to solve everything at once,
it is not going to work.

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:35.130
So think about this.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:38.790
The best compromise between user
experience and security risk.

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:42.990
Put KPIs, automatic ones,
and take action measure,

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:46.653
and put this process in place
to have another view about that.

00:12:46.653 --> 00:12:48.570
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Pascal,
I'd like to follow up

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:49.445
on that a little bit.

00:12:49.445 --> 00:12:52.150
You talked about using
KPIs to measure progress.

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:55.110
Can you talk a little about how
you use our tools, or perhaps

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:57.480
home-built tools, to
monitor those KPIs

00:12:57.480 --> 00:12:59.320
and keep an eye on risk?

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:00.920
PASCAL DALLA TORRE:
Yes, it's true

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:05.280
that we've been using
also Security Center,

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:09.030
like built-in
dashboards-- quite useful.

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:13.410
Of course, we have developed
also some productivity tools

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:16.490
in order to check a few
things that we didn't

00:13:16.490 --> 00:13:18.360
have natively with Google.

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:20.970
We are also using
investigation tool,

00:13:20.970 --> 00:13:23.580
which is a quite powerful
tool where we could just

00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:28.110
do some requests in order
to have some figures,

00:13:28.110 --> 00:13:29.650
as I mentioned before.

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:34.590
We've also been using
tools like Email Log Search

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:39.270
to confirm that there's some
traffic going in some SMTP

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:40.560
relays, for instance.

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:41.650
All those kinds of things.

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:44.580
So it's both a compromise
between the native tools

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:46.680
that Google could provide
and also the tools

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:48.993
that we have developed.

00:13:48.993 --> 00:13:50.410
ANDREW SULLIVAN:
Great, thank you.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.513
Ryan.

00:13:52.513 --> 00:13:53.180
RYAN SEIT: Sure.

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:56.690
So I would say
two-step verification,

00:13:56.690 --> 00:14:00.620
two-factor authentication,
day one, turn it on.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:03.560
Super important,
especially if you're

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:06.850
a larger company, or a company
of interest, or a disruptor.

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:11.010
Casper becomes a target for
many of our competition.

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:12.710
So we want to make
sure that we are

00:14:12.710 --> 00:14:16.580
taking the steps to mitigate
as many malicious attacks as

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:17.450
possible.

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:21.080
Two-step verification is a
great way to start doing that.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:27.060
Past that, incident management
protocols are super important.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:30.620
You don't really think
of a plan of action

00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:33.358
when something goes wrong
until something goes wrong.

00:14:33.358 --> 00:14:35.150
And by then, it's too
late, and you're just

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:39.490
operating on adrenaline, and
it just turns into a big mess.

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:42.530
Speaking from experience,
I've done that before

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:44.570
and learned a lot.

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:46.670
Being able to figure out
the course of action,

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:49.610
the parties that
need to be notified,

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:52.820
the notification schedule, and
having templates of information

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:55.370
that you're sending out to
the team with some variables

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:57.980
that you're changing, obviously,
so you're not reinventing

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:01.340
the wheel or writing an
entirely new email every time

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:04.430
you deal with a security issue.

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:05.867
I find that's super important.

00:15:05.867 --> 00:15:07.700
In addition to that, a
change advisory board

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:08.810
is super helpful.

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:10.610
When you're looking
to implement changes,

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:14.600
having an established change
advisory board with influencers

00:15:14.600 --> 00:15:16.670
in the company,
senior leadership,

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:18.270
you want those people involved.

00:15:18.270 --> 00:15:20.930
So when you do
recommend changes,

00:15:20.930 --> 00:15:22.400
they are vetted properly.

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:24.710
And then they
become, essentially,

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:33.162
the people who are championing
the change for you.

00:15:33.162 --> 00:15:34.620
So when it comes
from the top down,

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:39.007
it's adopted much, much
quicker by the end user.

00:15:39.007 --> 00:15:41.090
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Follow
up question for you, Ryan.

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:43.190
We talk a lot about
security keys.

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:45.410
We're very proud of the
fact that security keys

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:47.990
can help you reduce
the risk of phishing,

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:49.800
reduce the risk of
account hijacking.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.890
Sometimes, security
keys have a perception

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:53.330
of being difficult to use.

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:55.070
How did you overcome
that perception

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:57.250
among your user community?

00:15:57.250 --> 00:15:58.500
RYAN SEIT: That's a tough one.

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:01.380
So we're still rolling out
security keys to our fleet,

00:16:01.380 --> 00:16:04.910
but we do have a group of
outsourced contractors,

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:07.340
as well as our retail
teams, that are using

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:09.720
security keys on a daily basis.

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:12.350
Now, for new users who aren't
familiar with the protocols

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:13.475
of the company, super easy.

00:16:13.475 --> 00:16:15.392
Just give them the
security key and tell them,

00:16:15.392 --> 00:16:16.620
this is what you do.

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:21.212
But when it's a user who's
used to how it was before,

00:16:21.212 --> 00:16:22.670
it gets a little
bit more difficult

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:27.320
explaining to them the benefits
of utilizing security keys

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:29.540
for two-step authentication.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:33.350
There are other methods of
the two-step authentication

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:34.130
as well.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:36.350
Obviously, through your
phone, Google Authenticator.

00:16:36.350 --> 00:16:38.510
There are third
parties that do it too.

00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:43.520
But security keys I find
is a great first step

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:47.660
of that two-factor
authentication.

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:50.770
Not everybody is going to be
keen on carrying something

00:16:50.770 --> 00:16:52.020
around with them all the time.

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:56.000
So having the backup of a phone
for that verification process

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:57.140
is helpful.

00:16:57.140 --> 00:16:59.930
But we are working on it on
the corporate side, for sure.

00:16:59.930 --> 00:17:00.170
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Great.

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:00.730
Thank you.

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:03.380
Red, what's the one thing
every G Suite admin should

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:05.119
do to improve their security?

00:17:05.119 --> 00:17:07.490
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ: Oh,
look at it end-to-end.

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:08.960
I guess it's more of a--

00:17:08.960 --> 00:17:11.960
because my role actually looks
at devices and application--

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:16.010
so it's more of I can have an
end-to-end visibility with how

00:17:16.010 --> 00:17:18.140
the user experience is.

00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:20.089
I guess it's really
more of that.

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:21.810
So create a roadmap.

00:17:21.810 --> 00:17:27.290
Have the specific solution
each of the points

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:30.460
that you have to consider,
from the log-in experience

00:17:30.460 --> 00:17:35.390
to multi-factor authentication,
to MDM, up until the SSO

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:37.670
that you've implemented
in your application.

00:17:37.670 --> 00:17:39.800
So it's something that
we've been working on

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:42.170
over the last four years,
and we're trying to make sure

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:45.530
that we have that in place.

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:47.210
Also, I like what
you said earlier.

00:17:47.210 --> 00:17:49.550
I think that's true.

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:52.730
I'm going to add to something
what Ryan said earlier.

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:56.720
Having a change
management board.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:18:00.950
And at the same time, something
to do with our security

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:01.730
posture right now.

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:05.930
Since we're a teleco, we have
to comply with our version

00:18:05.930 --> 00:18:08.750
of GDPR, which is DPA.

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:10.910
We utilize the DPA
to make sure that we

00:18:10.910 --> 00:18:14.510
have funding if we need to put
more security layers on top

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:16.440
of what we have right now.

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:20.030
So use what you
have, and also make

00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:22.115
sure you have that
roadmap in place.

00:18:22.115 --> 00:18:24.490
ANDREW SULLIVAN: What's the
name of the regulation again?

00:18:24.490 --> 00:18:26.815
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ:
DPA, so Data Privacy App.

00:18:26.815 --> 00:18:28.690
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Can
you comment a little bit

00:18:28.690 --> 00:18:30.580
about how you do
your audits for DPA

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:34.200
and how you use tools from
us as well as your own

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:35.800
build tools for that?

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:38.110
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ: So
we have plenty of tools

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:40.630
that we're using, from
third-party solutions

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:44.800
to native Google solutions,
such as Security Center,

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:48.130
from both GCP and
also with G Suite.

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:51.310
But more so we also
have our internal team

00:18:51.310 --> 00:18:53.710
which is building not really
sophisticated but more

00:18:53.710 --> 00:18:57.010
of automating that
flow, from using Splunk

00:18:57.010 --> 00:19:00.840
to different
third-party solutions.

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:05.260
Yeah, we usually have
security incidents

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:07.780
that we can actually use
Security Center to make

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:11.380
sure we check all the
steps and also they

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:16.630
track the user's
usage, in a sense.

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:20.860
And that's how we utilize G
Suite Security Center, more

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:22.420
of the alerts as well.

00:19:22.420 --> 00:19:25.440
Actually, earlier today,
I received an alert

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:30.940
from Security Center stating
that this specific account has

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:33.730
been probably been
a phishing account.

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:39.550
But apparently part of our
Security Command Center team

00:19:39.550 --> 00:19:41.960
was actually testing
that functionality

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:44.590
that's abuse@globe.com.ph,
so basically it

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:49.170
sends an alert to everyone if
we notice some sort of abuse

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:57.060
for SMTP or Relay or
for any other activity.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:58.140
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Got it.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:19:58.400
Great.

00:19:58.400 --> 00:19:58.900
Thank you.

00:19:58.900 --> 00:20:01.230
So thinking about
best practices,

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:03.000
I'm going to ask a
few additional best

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:03.810
practice questions.

00:20:03.810 --> 00:20:06.900
But what are some of the
pitfalls, or blind spots,

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:08.760
or trouble spots
that you've run into?

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:11.830
And how are you able to overcome
them in the last few years?

00:20:14.128 --> 00:20:15.670
PASCAL DALLA TORRE:
Well, I'll start.

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:17.523
I keep on to respect the order.

00:20:17.523 --> 00:20:19.440
ANDREW SULLIVAN: We don't
have to go in order.

00:20:19.440 --> 00:20:21.232
We can go in a different
order, by the way.

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.470
Ryan, why don't you start.

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:28.970
We want to change it.

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:31.933
It's a little too orderly.

00:20:31.933 --> 00:20:33.350
RYAN SEIT: Well,
my initial answer

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:37.370
was we haven't really
run into too many issues.

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:39.770
Fingers crossed, all
those things crossed.

00:20:39.770 --> 00:20:43.250
I'm sure as soon as I said
that the place is on fire back

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:45.170
in New York.

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:52.790
But one thing that we've run
into is user authentication.

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:54.480
Not two-factor,
nothing like that.

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:56.010
But third-party services.

00:20:56.010 --> 00:20:59.090
So a lot of people still
use usernames and passwords

00:20:59.090 --> 00:21:02.048
for a service that is not
directly related to G Suite.

00:21:02.048 --> 00:21:04.340
The great thing about this,
and maybe I'm getting ahead

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:06.882
of myself a little bit-- this
is what happens when you put me

00:21:06.882 --> 00:21:08.630
on the spot--

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:13.490
using the native SSO
services from G Suite

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:18.570
to link that application
or service into G Suite

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:21.410
so it authenticates
through that,

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:23.900
mitigating the need for
a username, password.

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:26.150
Sometimes provisioning
a user automatically

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:28.250
and deprovisioning
them automatically,

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:31.735
that has alleviated a lot of
headache across the company

00:21:31.735 --> 00:21:33.860
where people were either
forgetting their usernames

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:36.950
or passwords, or just signing up
for services they shouldn't be

00:21:36.950 --> 00:21:42.760
and gaining access to services
that they shouldn't be.

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:44.510
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Pascal?

00:21:44.510 --> 00:21:49.050
PASCAL DALLA TORRE: OK,
so at Veolia, we faced,

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:50.740
nearly two years ago--

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:53.800
I think it was in May 2017--

00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:58.440
a large-scale phishing attack
directed at, basically, Google

00:21:58.440 --> 00:21:59.850
customers.

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:05.050
So as a classical
phishing attack,

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:08.400
the users received an email
from an attacker who pretended

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:11.010
to be a contact of one of them.

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:15.510
And it was basically a link to
an application which falsely

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:17.790
claimed to be a Google Doc.

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:21.030
And by this phishing
link, it was

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:24.240
supposed to access
to user accounts

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:28.020
in order to basically steal
all contact information.

00:22:28.020 --> 00:22:31.050
And then the idea behind it
was to send again this message

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:33.250
to other contacts and so on.

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:37.950
So Google did its job disabling
all the links, removing

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:39.790
the log-in page and so on.

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:42.930
So what we have
learned from that,

00:22:42.930 --> 00:22:45.900
basically, is that first of
all, we can use the customized

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:47.190
settings from Google.

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:50.550
We have some that we have
applied at the domain level,

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:56.640
like identifying links behind
shortened URLs, for instance.

00:22:56.640 --> 00:23:00.030
It's a bit technical, but it's
one feature you could put.

00:23:00.030 --> 00:23:05.160
Also related to scan linked
images, things like that.

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:07.770
And also some
warning prompt when

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:11.220
you click on a link that
goes to an untrusted domain,

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:14.160
then all of a sudden
pops up a warning, which

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:15.910
is quite efficient as well.

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:19.060
So those are basically the
tools that Google can provide

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:21.220
and you can put in place.

00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:23.610
The second part I
would mention is--

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:26.340
I come back to a point
that Ryan already

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:29.610
talked about-- is
related to security keys.

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:32.970
So security keys,
it's probably today

00:23:32.970 --> 00:23:36.510
the best second-step
verification method

00:23:36.510 --> 00:23:39.930
that we have against phishing.

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:42.340
Better than Prompt
Authenticator and so on.

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:45.180
So I mentioned before that
it was one of the key points

00:23:45.180 --> 00:23:48.570
for Veolia that time in 2013.

00:23:48.570 --> 00:23:50.760
Even though we have
evolved and the users we

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:52.620
recommend that
they use prompt, we

00:23:52.620 --> 00:23:55.110
have a very
sensitive population,

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:57.370
which are super admin accounts.

00:23:57.370 --> 00:23:59.460
So we have the G Suite
super admin accounts.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:01.230
We have the GCP
super admin accounts.

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:05.040
We have all the Cloud
platform super admin accounts.

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:08.640
And the third population
is also all super admins

00:24:08.640 --> 00:24:11.040
related to SES
solution that we have

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.210
integrated in our environment.

00:24:13.210 --> 00:24:16.300
I mentioned like for CRM,
HR, payroll, and so on.

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:19.120
And we have very
sensitive information.

00:24:19.120 --> 00:24:21.240
So what we've done so
far, we have identified

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:26.970
those populations, and we
started to actually create

00:24:26.970 --> 00:24:29.010
a dedicated G Suite account.

00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:30.780
Needless to mention
that some of them

00:24:30.780 --> 00:24:32.700
were using their regular
accounts in order

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:34.520
to do some sensitive action.

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:38.160
But I'm sure Veolia is not
the only company, between us.

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:40.830
So we started to roll
out security keys.

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:42.490
So pay attention to one point.

00:24:42.490 --> 00:24:45.270
And maybe we can talk
about that again later.

00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:48.270
The security now that has
been provided by Google,

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:50.400
the Titan key with
the Bluetooth mode

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:55.590
is very good as user experience.

00:24:55.590 --> 00:24:57.930
And nobody can claim
that they have an issue

00:24:57.930 --> 00:25:01.410
because the security cannot be
introduced in their Chromebooks

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:02.800
or other devices.

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:07.410
And so it does not work anymore
since it works contactless,

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:08.670
which is very good.

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:10.050
And the last point is--

00:25:10.050 --> 00:25:14.730
don't give in to people who come
to you and say, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:25:14.730 --> 00:25:18.570
but today I have
forgotten my security key.

00:25:18.570 --> 00:25:21.210
It was much easier
with Prompt or SMS.

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:24.540
You know, I always have
my telephone with me.

00:25:24.540 --> 00:25:26.940
And I tell them that
when you go shopping,

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:28.900
you take your credit
card, don't you?

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:31.113
And if you just forget
once your credit

00:25:31.113 --> 00:25:32.280
card, the second time, sure.

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:33.280
You won't forget it.

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:34.900
And it's the same
with a security key.

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:36.560
And this is what
we have been doing.

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:41.970
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ:
On our end--

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:44.480
I have two things, actually.

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:46.600
One is MDM and the
other one was SSO.

00:25:46.600 --> 00:25:50.550
So about probably
three years ago,

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:55.730
so we've started the venture
of trying to introduce MDM

00:25:55.730 --> 00:25:57.560
to our employees.

00:25:57.560 --> 00:26:02.450
The thing is, we were kind of
having hesitation from them.

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:05.905
Specifically because it's
going to be on their device.

00:26:05.905 --> 00:26:07.280
First question
was, are you going

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:08.930
to check my personal files?

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:12.613
Or all of these things that
they're more conscious of,

00:26:12.613 --> 00:26:13.280
and that's good.

00:26:13.280 --> 00:26:17.010
They're more conscious of what
happens to their accounts.

00:26:17.010 --> 00:26:19.700
But it took them
a while to realize

00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:23.210
that it's better to have the
MDM in place rather than not.

00:26:23.210 --> 00:26:27.230
So there was an event
three years ago.

00:26:27.230 --> 00:26:31.310
One of top guys in
Globe lost their phone

00:26:31.310 --> 00:26:33.560
while they were in Barcelona.

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:36.370
So they're doing the MWC event.

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:39.710
So they were there, and the
first thing that they did

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:41.690
is actually they contacted me.

00:26:41.690 --> 00:26:45.140
And it was nighttime
in Manila at the time.

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:47.010
And I had to do something.

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:50.090
And I told them, did you
enable MDM on your end?

00:26:50.090 --> 00:26:52.010
Or they were able to set it up?

00:26:52.010 --> 00:26:53.183
And they said, yes.

00:26:53.183 --> 00:26:54.350
And I said yeah, good thing.

00:26:54.350 --> 00:26:58.115
Because we're able
to lock the phone.

00:26:58.115 --> 00:27:03.110
And actually he was the one
who championed MDM across Globe

00:27:03.110 --> 00:27:04.730
because of that experience.

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:07.610
And we kind of made
sure that everyone

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:08.750
knew about what happened.

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:11.930
And in the leader circle,
they were very well aware

00:27:11.930 --> 00:27:14.090
of what's going to
happen if they don't turn

00:27:14.090 --> 00:27:16.640
on their MDM on their phones.

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:19.310
The second one was the SSO.

00:27:19.310 --> 00:27:25.080
So right now,
because of the road

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:27.050
map that we have
for the experience,

00:27:27.050 --> 00:27:30.090
people are now focusing
on all their applications.

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:32.090
They want to have the SSO
experience across all.

00:27:32.090 --> 00:27:34.130
And they want
Google login there.

00:27:34.130 --> 00:27:38.620
Because before Google login, we
have our own internal solution

00:27:38.620 --> 00:27:42.710
that was supposed to be
doing that functionality.

00:27:42.710 --> 00:27:45.830
But because of
albeit an old one,

00:27:45.830 --> 00:27:50.930
and it's not something that
was able to accommodate

00:27:50.930 --> 00:27:53.857
all the functionality
that we requested,

00:27:53.857 --> 00:27:55.940
they were having a hard
time logging in, or rather

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:59.720
integrating their
application to that solution.

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:02.960
So it was something that
we wanted to push for it.

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:06.630
So right now, all of our
software SSO service solutions,

00:28:06.630 --> 00:28:07.970
they're all using Google login.

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:13.100
And since then, we haven't
experienced any account

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:15.360
compromise whatsoever.

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:18.890
We just made sure that everyone
gets two FA at the onset,

00:28:18.890 --> 00:28:21.410
and we disable those things
that should be disabled.

00:28:21.410 --> 00:28:24.260
For example, IMAP
and POP3 disabled

00:28:24.260 --> 00:28:27.455
at the start of
the account itself,

00:28:27.455 --> 00:28:28.580
or creation of the account.

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:29.370
ANDREW SULLIVAN:
That's a common theme.

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:30.920
Disable IMAP and POP3.

00:28:30.920 --> 00:28:33.560
You can do that with one
checkbox, by the way.

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:36.260
I noticed that both
Ryan and Red were

00:28:36.260 --> 00:28:39.740
talking about enabling single
sign-on to third-party services

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:41.300
using Google Cloud Identity.

00:28:41.300 --> 00:28:42.800
Were there some
barriers or problems

00:28:42.800 --> 00:28:44.342
you had to overcome
when you did that

00:28:44.342 --> 00:28:47.328
with either user resistance or
difficulty with the services?

00:28:47.328 --> 00:28:48.370
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ: No.

00:28:48.370 --> 00:28:49.203
ANDREW SULLIVAN: No.

00:28:49.203 --> 00:28:49.703
Right?

00:28:49.703 --> 00:28:51.370
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ:
I guess everyone's

00:28:51.370 --> 00:28:53.560
familiar with the
Google login, so it's

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:56.990
like they're using it that
their own personal accounts.

00:28:56.990 --> 00:28:59.840
I guess what we did
so that it was easy

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:04.250
for them was to emulate how they
do it in their own accounts.

00:29:04.250 --> 00:29:07.250
In that same way, once
we enabled, for example,

00:29:07.250 --> 00:29:10.978
2FA in the SSO, they
wanted to do it as well

00:29:10.978 --> 00:29:12.270
on their own personal accounts.

00:29:12.270 --> 00:29:15.710
So we were able
to influence them

00:29:15.710 --> 00:29:18.320
and made sure that they're
well aware of security risk

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:19.927
on their own space.

00:29:19.927 --> 00:29:22.010
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Well, I
noticed a couple of cases

00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:24.760
where actually your end user
was a security advocate.

00:29:24.760 --> 00:29:27.680
Your end user said, hey, I was
able to improve my security

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:29.750
on my own account,
or in the other case,

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:33.650
your implementation of MDM was
able to save me a security risk

00:29:33.650 --> 00:29:34.700
personally.

00:29:34.700 --> 00:29:36.380
So was that a case
where they were

00:29:36.380 --> 00:29:40.670
able to actually be an advocate
throughout the organization?

00:29:40.670 --> 00:29:42.020
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ: Yes.

00:29:42.020 --> 00:29:46.190
Also, for some reason, we're
treating them as babies

00:29:46.190 --> 00:29:46.690
as well.

00:29:46.690 --> 00:29:50.960
So that's one of the mandates
really, of Globe telecom.

00:29:50.960 --> 00:29:52.880
We want to make sure
that our employees are

00:29:52.880 --> 00:29:54.140
being treated properly.

00:29:54.140 --> 00:29:57.730
So instead of just telling
them exactly what to do,

00:29:57.730 --> 00:29:59.540
or trying to influence
them as well,

00:29:59.540 --> 00:30:01.880
that they have to do it on
their own personal space too.

00:30:01.880 --> 00:30:05.570
So for example, there were
cases before where Facebook

00:30:05.570 --> 00:30:07.170
accounts-- not even
Google accounts--

00:30:07.170 --> 00:30:10.080
Facebook accounts on their own
personal space got compromised.

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:12.583
And we told them to
enable 2FA as well.

00:30:12.583 --> 00:30:14.750
So it's not really about
just Google and whatsoever.

00:30:14.750 --> 00:30:16.910
It's about security
posture across all.

00:30:16.910 --> 00:30:19.220
So at the end of the
day, user, whether it's

00:30:19.220 --> 00:30:21.230
a personal space
or a company space,

00:30:21.230 --> 00:30:23.120
would have the
same security risk.

00:30:26.460 --> 00:30:27.910
ANDREW SULLIVAN: Great.

00:30:27.910 --> 00:30:28.410
OK.

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:30.340
Next question.

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:33.000
We wanted you to share
some tips and tricks.

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:38.340
Things that you do that make
your management experience

00:30:38.340 --> 00:30:40.770
with Google Admin better
or that save you time

00:30:40.770 --> 00:30:42.838
while administering
G Suite security.

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:45.810
Sure.

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:46.358
Red.

00:30:46.358 --> 00:30:47.650
Yeah, Red, you start this time.

00:30:50.298 --> 00:30:52.090
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ:
Well, keep it simple.

00:30:52.090 --> 00:30:55.230
What we're doing is go
for the native ones.

00:30:55.230 --> 00:30:58.200
If we're not satisfied with
it, we go to automate it

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:00.780
and to get third-party
party solutions.

00:31:00.780 --> 00:31:03.780
But as much as possible, we want
to go for the native solutions

00:31:03.780 --> 00:31:08.620
and try to lock as soon as
possible before we can--

00:31:08.620 --> 00:31:11.070
for example, with
the IMAP and POP3.

00:31:11.070 --> 00:31:12.670
At the onset, we
had to disable it,

00:31:12.670 --> 00:31:15.100
and not just on G Suite level,
but on the network level.

00:31:15.100 --> 00:31:18.360
So even if the
administrator mistakenly

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:20.060
turned on the IMAP
and POP3, there

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:21.810
are certain floors in the
building that are really

00:31:21.810 --> 00:31:22.768
locked down across all.

00:31:22.768 --> 00:31:26.700
So you cannot use
your IMAP and POP3.

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:31.380
Also, in terms of--

00:31:31.380 --> 00:31:33.510
you have to make sure
you have user templates.

00:31:33.510 --> 00:31:35.840
I like what they
were saying earlier.

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:37.620
We do that as well
in Globe because we

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:40.450
have a different security team.

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:42.840
We call them ISDP, so
Information, Security, and Data

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:43.740
Privacy team.

00:31:43.740 --> 00:31:48.330
And our team is this
information systems group.

00:31:48.330 --> 00:31:50.800
So basically, we work
hand-in-hand with them.

00:31:50.800 --> 00:31:53.070
So there's operations,
and at the same time,

00:31:53.070 --> 00:31:54.630
they maintain their policies.

00:31:54.630 --> 00:31:57.270
But we are more and
focusing on the solutions

00:31:57.270 --> 00:31:59.350
and implementation
of these solutions.

00:31:59.350 --> 00:32:02.220
So as I mentioned
earlier, the templates

00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:04.890
that we collaborated with--

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:08.300
it was easy for us to give
awareness to our employees

00:32:08.300 --> 00:32:11.830
to the point that, probably,
they're sick of our awareness

00:32:11.830 --> 00:32:15.240
already in terms of templates
and use cases and whatever.

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:19.410
But it was helpful for
us to veer them away

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:22.358
from the usual to more of
a higher security posture.

00:32:22.358 --> 00:32:24.150
ANDREW SULLIVAN: And
tell me a little more.

00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:27.090
When you say security
templates, what do you mean?

00:32:27.090 --> 00:32:28.680
RED JAQUES BERNALDEZ:
For example,

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:33.210
when you have new hacker
use or whatever issues,

00:32:33.210 --> 00:32:36.000
we have a template ready
that we can already

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:37.470
use to communicate fast.

00:32:37.470 --> 00:32:38.250
That's one.

00:32:38.250 --> 00:32:43.330
To announce new upgrades or
functionalities, that's two.

00:32:43.330 --> 00:32:49.410
The third one would be, in terms
of requesting for security.

00:32:49.410 --> 00:32:51.900
For example, for
SSO, where you have

00:32:51.900 --> 00:32:53.840
to create a project
in GCP, you have

00:32:53.840 --> 00:32:56.340
to integrate it with
a project for you

00:32:56.340 --> 00:33:00.910
to use the admin APIs of Google.

00:33:00.910 --> 00:33:03.630
You have to request
it in a manner,

00:33:03.630 --> 00:33:08.310
and you have to explain what
these applications, what

00:33:08.310 --> 00:33:10.690
requests, what use
the API is for.

00:33:10.690 --> 00:33:12.780
So what we do is to
create that template,

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:15.030
make sure that the
file it properly to us,

00:33:15.030 --> 00:33:18.210
and it's a collaborative
template, actually.

00:33:18.210 --> 00:33:22.200
So all of our security
personnel would

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:25.860
be able to go through that
template and give their advice,

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:27.660
whether we go through
it with it or not.

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:33.860
PASCAL DALLA TORRE:
Yes, for Veolia,

00:33:33.860 --> 00:33:37.820
I would say three major
pillars and tips and tricks.

00:33:37.820 --> 00:33:39.240
SSO, we mentioned it.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:44.900
MDM with the basic ones inside
like pin codes, green locks,

00:33:44.900 --> 00:33:46.590
and all that stuff.

00:33:46.590 --> 00:33:48.410
And again, two-step
verification.

00:33:48.410 --> 00:33:52.070
And again, you really
have to carry on

00:33:52.070 --> 00:33:54.530
with this, which is very
important, as we mentioned

00:33:54.530 --> 00:33:55.320
before.

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:57.140
Otherwise, you can
explain to your user

00:33:57.140 --> 00:33:59.490
that they will have to change
the password on a very,

00:33:59.490 --> 00:34:03.790
very, very regular basis, which
can be a pain in the neck.

00:34:03.790 --> 00:34:10.590
So maybe a tip to maybe
roll out more broader

00:34:10.590 --> 00:34:12.920
this is kind of
two-step verification

00:34:12.920 --> 00:34:15.320
is, first of all, to
start with a scope

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:17.090
that you have under control.

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:18.179
People that you know.

00:34:18.179 --> 00:34:21.350
One or two key
players that you have.

00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:22.909
And you start to
discuss with them.

00:34:22.909 --> 00:34:25.429
If they have influence, you
can go and have meetings

00:34:25.429 --> 00:34:28.850
with their team explaining why
it's the best way to do it.

00:34:28.850 --> 00:34:33.770
And start to elaborate a clear
and understandable process.

00:34:33.770 --> 00:34:35.840
Like you identified
the population,

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:38.330
you distribute your
keys, you spend some time

00:34:38.330 --> 00:34:42.810
with some users trying to set up
the keys within their accounts.

00:34:42.810 --> 00:34:45.530
And I mentioned before
the super admin accounts.

00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:46.440
What you do is--

00:34:46.440 --> 00:34:48.920
and it's now available
within Google--

00:34:48.920 --> 00:34:51.920
you say that this two-step
verification is security key

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:53.630
and only security key.

00:34:53.630 --> 00:34:59.300
And the second point is that
you [INAUDIBLE] trusted device.

00:34:59.300 --> 00:35:01.130
It means that each
time they login,

00:35:01.130 --> 00:35:04.020
they have to play the
security key, no other choice.

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:07.620
So this is really, really great
for those sensitive accounts.

00:35:07.620 --> 00:35:12.020
So again, it's
really basic, but it

00:35:12.020 --> 00:35:14.462
has to be implemented in
order to enforce security.

00:35:14.462 --> 00:35:15.920
ANDREW SULLIVAN:
What I notice here

00:35:15.920 --> 00:35:24.200
is this is as much about process
and about putting together

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:26.840
an organizational approach
that gets people involved

00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:29.540
in securing their own devices,
rather than just using

00:35:29.540 --> 00:35:31.263
the technical solutions.

00:35:31.263 --> 00:35:32.930
PASCAL DALLA TORRE:
Well, it is correct.

00:35:32.930 --> 00:35:36.230
I mean, a large-scale
company like ours,

00:35:36.230 --> 00:35:39.080
and I guess it's also a good
playground even with smaller

00:35:39.080 --> 00:35:41.240
companies, there
is no other choice.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:44.010
If you don't do that, then
it's going to be a total mess.

00:35:44.010 --> 00:35:45.770
I mean, I don't see
any options if you

00:35:45.770 --> 00:35:47.595
don't have a clear,
understandable process.

00:35:47.595 --> 00:35:49.220
ANDREW SULLIVAN: I
couldn't agree more.

00:35:49.220 --> 00:35:50.720
Ryan?

00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:53.420
RYAN SEIT: Yeah, to
elaborate a little bit more

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:55.670
on what Pascal mentioned
earlier, service

00:35:55.670 --> 00:35:57.650
accounts for everything.

00:35:57.650 --> 00:36:01.430
Anything that requires an
admin log in or account

00:36:01.430 --> 00:36:03.960
to do something
administratively,

00:36:03.960 --> 00:36:05.210
have a service account for it.

00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:08.010
Don't tie it to a
personal account.

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:11.270
I think continuing along those
lines, Team Drives is great.

00:36:11.270 --> 00:36:13.160
Google Team Drives
allows you to--

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:14.720
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:14.720 --> 00:36:17.180
Let's hear it for
Google Team Drives.

00:36:17.180 --> 00:36:17.720
All right.

00:36:17.720 --> 00:36:18.220
Nice.

00:36:20.930 --> 00:36:23.540
I'll just drop the mic on that.

00:36:23.540 --> 00:36:25.040
Yeah, so Google
Team Drives is great

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:29.510
because it allows you to remove
the actual file from being

00:36:29.510 --> 00:36:31.160
attached to a particular user.

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:34.110
User leaves the company, those
files don't go with them.

00:36:34.110 --> 00:36:36.590
Although there are methods
of getting around that,

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:40.220
Team Drives is a
great tool that we

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:43.310
are starting to implement
across the entire company

00:36:43.310 --> 00:36:44.770
to great success.

00:36:44.770 --> 00:36:48.420
Past that, automate
as much as you can.

00:36:48.420 --> 00:36:51.320
There are so many things that
we need to do as administrators.

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:52.800
It's very tedious work.

00:36:52.800 --> 00:36:56.870
And as you scale, I mean I'm
only speaking for myself.

00:36:56.870 --> 00:36:59.270
I'm sure everybody
else on this stage

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:02.030
knows far more better
than I do about this.

00:37:02.030 --> 00:37:04.940
But those processes start to
scale when you multiply them

00:37:04.940 --> 00:37:07.260
by 10, 20, 30, 40 50%.

00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:09.810
It just becomes a nightmare.

00:37:09.810 --> 00:37:10.820
So automate.

00:37:10.820 --> 00:37:12.140
Google Apps Script.

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:13.940
Fantastic tool.

00:37:13.940 --> 00:37:16.490
We use that for onboarding
and offboarding,

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:19.620
so our HR teams are able
to enter information,

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:22.580
the relative name for the user
or the manager of the user,

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:25.333
the start date, if it's a
contract at the end date.

00:37:25.333 --> 00:37:27.500
And then it kicks off
automation sending [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:31.740
to the hiring manager,
creating the actual accounts.

00:37:31.740 --> 00:37:33.860
We use a third-party tool
cart called BetterCloud,

00:37:33.860 --> 00:37:36.920
which is a fantastic one that
integrates very tightly with G

00:37:36.920 --> 00:37:40.520
Suite that allows us to kick
off workflows or automation.

00:37:40.520 --> 00:37:42.920
So it'll work with
Slack, et cetera,

00:37:42.920 --> 00:37:45.770
and just create all the
accounts we need for day one

00:37:45.770 --> 00:37:47.180
when that user starts.

00:37:47.180 --> 00:37:49.080
Very little interaction
from our part.

00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:52.030
And then conversely, when
they leave, same situation.

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:07.270
It removes everything and
just makes life a lot easier,

00:38:07.270 --> 00:38:09.600
and ensures there's
no user error there.

00:38:09.600 --> 00:38:11.363
So automation, huge thing.

00:38:11.363 --> 00:38:12.780
ANDREW SULLIVAN:
Really appreciate

00:38:12.780 --> 00:38:13.822
that with the automation.

00:38:13.822 --> 00:38:17.310
Who uses Apps Script
on a regular basis?

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:18.700
Cool.

